{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmMMbrux3eUtfEe47DQt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, `take_the_best_streak_switch_balance`, and it passed the self-check (`OK`), but on the data it fits far worse than the best model. Its smoke-fit ELPD-LOO is about −4486, against −3565.5 for the best model, `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip`. That puts it roughly 900 nats behind, in the same range as the other simple-cue models in the set.\n\n**Hypothesis.** This is a decision-strategy model, which no current model has. People compare the two sequences one cue at a time, in a fixed order, and the first cue that clearly separates them decides. The order is:\n1. Longest streak: the sequence with the shorter longest run looks more random.\n2. If the streaks look alike, number of heads/tails switches.\n3. If that also looks alike, heads/tails balance.\n\nIf no cue separates the pair, the person's own side habit decides. Because switching is checked before balance, a clear difference in switching wins over a difference in balance. This targets the critique that people pick the more-switching sequence even when it is more lopsided.\n\n**Model.** How likely a cue is to be noticed rises smoothly with how much the two sequences differ on it, so there is no hard threshold. Each cue has its own fitted reliability, which can be positive or negative. On top of that are each person's commitment (heavy-tailed, and allowed to reverse) and their side habit. The features come from `compute_features`, so the model can be scored on the novelty pool.\n\n**Self-check warnings:**\n- **Unreliable PSIS-LOO:** 3.2% of trials (248 of 7744) have Pareto k > 0.5, above the 1% tolerance. Admission may therefore mark the fit as untrusted.\n- **Convergence not verified:** the smoke fit used one chain, so R-hat couldn't be computed. Novelty against the other models also wasn't checked.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/ku